HOW THIS ROW WAS FOUND, and what was refuted on the way.

The bead I opened (br-r37-c1-weighted-attr-rust-store-237hw) claims a
"~0.6-0.72x floor on all weight= ops". THAT CLAIM IS REFUTED on HEAD. Measured
live, 35/40 rows decidable: size(weight) 26.3x-43.7x, degree(weight) whole
1.70x-4.25x, degree(nbunch,weight) 0.74x-2.86x, size() unweighted 204x-2833x.
There is no 0.6-0.72x floor left to close. The survey did surface something the
bead never described - the SCALAR form of the same operation, at 0.10x.

FIRST HYPOTHESIS WRONG, KILLED BY THE PROBE. A 10x loss on a ONE-NODE request
looked like whole-graph materialization. It is not. Holding the request FIXED
(node 0, always exactly 4 incident edges) and growing the PARENT 500 -> 8000
nodes, fnx's cost is FLAT at 0.97-1.02x, exactly as networkx's is. A constant
factor, not a complexity class: graph.adj[node] materialises the row through
the AtlasView chain and a nested generator then touches every parallel edge.

SECOND MEASUREMENT WRONG, CAUGHT BY REDOING IT. A first timing suggested routing
would HURT the simple classes (Graph 0.351x -> 0.259x). That was my own probe's
dict() construction and indexing, not the kernel. The raw kernel call is 1.007us
against a 2.393us scalar path, so routing helps all four classes.

EXACTNESS WAS THE GATE, NOT SPEED. CPython's builtins.sum is Neumaier-
compensated, and this bead's own comment history records that trap being caught
during feasibility - a Rust fold that is merely close is a parity bug. The
kernel is compensation-correct, verified on a case that DISCRIMINATES: weights
[1e16, 1.0, -1e16] give 1.0 under builtins.sum and 0.0 under a naive
left-to-right fold, and the kernel returns 1.0 on all four classes. 98 scalar
cases agree with networkx exactly - ints past i64, bool, missing weight,
self-loops, parallel self-loops, inf, -inf, nan, mixed int-then-float result
TYPE, str and None weight values, isolates, a non-default weight key.

DIRECTION IS THE DANGEROUS PART. The native kernel answers TOTAL degree, so an
in_degree or out_degree view routed to it would return a WRONG NUMBER rather
than a slow one. The simple-class site is gated on `_direction is None`; the
multigraph funnel is reached only from total-degree view classes, which carry no
_direction at all. 84 directed checks pass, on fixtures where in != out != total
so the assertion is not vacuous.

